2026-06-30 15:17:42,483 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-140d8e3b-bd64-49e1-97f2-266074e050d8', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nLumos_200323_100.raw\nLumos_200323_101.raw\nLumos_200323_102.raw\nLumos_200323_98.raw\nLumos_200323_99.raw\nLumos_200421_08.raw\nLumos_200421_09.raw\nLumos_200421_10.raw\nLumos_200421_11.raw\nLumos_200421_12.raw\nLumos_200421_13.raw\nLumos_200421_14.raw\nLumos_200421_15.raw\nLumos_200421_16.raw\nLumos_200421_17.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nBioID-screening reveals the Alk “proximitome” of the Drosophila brain\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAnaplastic lymphoma kinase (Alk) is an evolutionary conserved receptor tyrosine kinase of the insulin receptor family. In addition to its well-studied role in cancer, numerous studies on invertebrate and vertebrate models reveal that Alk-signaling is associated with a variety of complex traits such as: regulation of growth and metabolism, hibernation, regulation of neurotransmitters, synaptic coupling, axon targeting, decision making, memory formation and learning, alcohol use disorder, as well as steroid hormone metabolism. To shed light on ALK-driven signaling processes, we used BioID-based in vivo proximity labeling to reveal the molecules that interact with Alk in the Drosophila CNS. Therefore, we generated first and next generation BioID fusion proteins in the endogenous Alk locus by CRISPR/Cas9 induced homology-directed repair (HDR) resulting in viable and fertile flies. Our results show (i) that the next generation of BioID proteins (TurboID and miniTurbo) outperform the first generation BirA* fusion in terms of labeling efficiency, and (ii) allow identification of proximitomes without overexpression which are likely to be more physiologically relevant. LC-MS/MS-based BioID screening of the larval brain revealed an extensive neuronal Alk-proximitome identifying numerous novel potential components of Alk signaling complexes. Validation of Alk-proximitome candidates further revealed co-expression with Alk in the CNS.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe streptavidin beads with attached proteins were washed twice with 1 ml 50 mM triethylammonium bicarbonate (TEAB) and digested with 0.5 µg LysC Mass Spec grade (Promega) in 100 µl 50 mM TEAB at 37 °C for 3h with flipping the tubes every 30 min. The samples were reduced in 5 mM tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine (TCEP) for 30 min at room temperature and alkylated in 10 mM methyl methanethiosulfonate (MMTS) for 30 min at room temperature. A second digestion was performed with 0.3 µg Pierce MS grade Trypsin (Thermo Fisher Scientific) at 37°C overnight. Next morning the digested samples were removed from beads with a magnetic stand and the beads were washed twice with 50 µl 50 mM TEAB that were pooled with the supernatants. Digested peptides were labelled using Tandem Mass Tag reagents (TMTpro, Thermo Fisher Scientific) according to the manufacturer´s instructions. The combined set was pre-fractionated into 5 and 10 fractions using Pierce High pH Reversed-Phase Peptide Fractionation Kit (Thermo Scientific) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. The fractions were dried in a vacuum centrifuge and reconstituted in 3% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid for analysis. The fractions were analyzed on an Orbitrap Fusion™ Lumos™ Tribrid™ mass spectrometer interfaced with an Easy-nLC1200 liquid chromatography system (both Thermo Fisher Scientific). Peptides were trapped on an Acclaim Pepmap 100 C18 trap column (100 μm x 2 cm, particle size 5 μm, Thermo Fischer Scientific) and separated on an in-house packed analytical column i.d. 75 μm, particle size 3 μm, Reprosil-Pur C18, Dr. Maisch, length 35 cm using a linear gradient from 5% to 33% B over 77 min followed by an increase to 100% B for 3 min, and 100% B for 10 min at a flow of 300 nL/min. Solvent A was 0.2% formic acid and solvent B was 80% acetonitrile, 0.2% formic acid. Precursor ion mass spectra were acquired at 120 000 resolution and MS/MS analysis was performed in a data-dependent multinotch mode where CID spectra of the most intense precursor ions were recorded in the ion trap with collision energy setting of 30 for 3 s (‘top speed’ setting). Precursors were isolated in the quadrupole with a 0.7 m/z isolation window, charge states 2 to 7 were selected for fragmentation, dynamic exclusion was set to 45 s and 10 ppm. MS3 spectra for reporter ion quantitation were recorded at 50 000 resolution with HCD fragmentation at collision energy of and 55 using the synchronous precursor selection.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nDrosophila melanogaster (fruit fly)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
